research/audit/wave3-judge-adjudications.jsonl:90:{"id":"lem-rational-power-laws","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"ca57e2608f3189f9888a2fcbeb71a871a6910e9bf152118253a34f08ac10476b","item_sha256":"e4fd52b4329672178780982168b00b71092ce937411a8904a5d5c5288e0f5ea2","outcome":"confirmed_fatal","defect_type":"dependency_citation","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"[L6] reads \"0^t = 0 for every rational t > 0, and 0^t is left undefined for t <= 0\" while its cited def-rational-power says the opposite at t = 0: \"For r < 0 the expression 0^r is left undefined\" and \"The convention 0^0 = 1 of def-integer-power is untouched: r = 0 is not covered by the supplementary clause ... So 0^0 = 1 while 0^r = 0 for every rational r > 0.\" The Fact contradicts its source on a case the source legislates explicitly, which is the lem-divisor-bound pattern I ruled fatal at A6. Not load-bearing (steps 3.4 and 4.1 use only t > 0), so the repair is confined to [L6]."}
research/audit/wave3-judge-adjudications.jsonl:94:{"id":"def-rational-power","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"feb112313178b95226c75ba4919be180669dbff645ad4374dc7db923dff9d876","item_sha256":"cc19733b3d73db102b644c85c83b8275b69a18aa84a3985549c37e71f11dc615","outcome":"confirmed_fatal","defect_type":"other","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"Published-claim decay, and the item cites the very page that falsifies it. The third remark asserts \"There is no $a^{x}$ for irrational $x$ in this library\", and rem-real-exponents-deferred - cited in the next sentence, and repaired at A6 to record the later publication - now ends \"That later development is now published: [[def-real-power]] defines $a^{x} := \\exp(x \\log a)$ for $a > 0$ and real $x$, and [[thm-real-power-laws]] proves the power laws for those exponents.\" A false claim about the library in reader-visible prose, contradicted by its own citation. Same class as A6-F5."}
research/audit/wave3-judge-adjudications.jsonl:108:{"id":"rem-connectedness-conventions","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"217deb27640bde6599bc2c67aca3745b6936188485ab937518637e149fce26dd","item_sha256":"0cafce2cb0e757fac888589bde6bf55058ae4b2717670ad71e11e355cb3c63cf","outcome":"confirmed_fatal","defect_type":"other","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"Section 3 misstates a named literature notion and describes a vacuous condition as a weaker one. It reads \"Dropping the word *open* from V gives a weaker condition at a point, called *connectedness im kleinen* in the literature\", but a connected V with x in V contained in U is satisfied by V = {x} for every x and every U, so the stripped condition holds in every space. Connectedness im kleinen at x asks for a connected NEIGHBOURHOOD V of x inside U - a distinction this item's own inherited-conventions paragraph (\"a neighbourhood need not be open\") makes expressible. Nothing in the item rescues the sentence."}
